WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.683
<v Tutor>Good morning, and welcome to Part Two Daily.

2
00:00:02.683 --> 00:00:09.133
<v Tutor>It's Saturday the fourteenth of November, one hundred and sixteen days until the written paper on the tenth of March.

3
00:00:09.133 --> 00:00:11.094
<v Tutor>Saturday is the spiral review.

4
00:00:11.094 --> 00:00:19.195
<v Tutor>We go back to topics from the last few weeks, rebuild the answer that earns the marks, and then make each one harder by linking it to another system.

5
00:00:19.195 --> 00:00:26.264
<v Tutor>Spaced, harder retrieval is what makes things stick, and it's the closest thing to how the College actually writes its questions.

6
00:00:26.264 --> 00:00:28.224
<v Tutor>There are four revisits today.

7
00:00:28.224 --> 00:00:32.662
<v Tutor>First, rhabdomyolysis, with an exertional heat stroke and hyperkalaemia twist.

8
00:00:32.662 --> 00:00:37.254
<v Tutor>Second, end-stage kidney disease in the critically ill, with septic shock on top.

9
00:00:37.254 --> 00:00:41.640
<v Tutor>Third, hypernatraemia and diabetes insipidus, with a renal replacement twist.

10
00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:46.593
<v Tutor>Fourth, the low output state after cardiac surgery, with the right ventricle joining in.

11
00:00:46.593 --> 00:00:51.908
<v Tutor>Then a syllabus round-up of six items, from mechanical cardiac support to antimicrobial dosing.

12
00:00:51.908 --> 00:00:54.127
<v Tutor>There's no journal watch this week.

13
00:00:54.127 --> 00:00:59.080
<v Tutor>Nothing new and important has appeared since the last one, so we'll leave that part out.

14
00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:03.054
<v Tutor>Let's start in London, in September 1940.

15
00:01:03.054 --> 00:01:04.524
<v Tutor>The Blitz has begun.

16
00:01:04.524 --> 00:01:10.092
<v Tutor>Rescue teams are pulling people out of collapsed buildings, some after hours trapped under rubble.

17
00:01:10.092 --> 00:01:13.401
<v Tutor>Many of them look surprisingly well when they come out.

18
00:01:13.401 --> 00:01:18.390
<v Tutor>A trapped leg, some swelling, but they're talking, and their blood pressure is holding.

19
00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:20.964
<v Tutor>Then, hours later, some of them collapse.

20
00:01:20.964 --> 00:01:23.485
<v Tutor>Fluid is pouring into the injured limbs.

21
00:01:23.485 --> 00:01:28.212
<v Tutor>Over the next few days their urine turns dark and smoky, and then it nearly stops.

22
00:01:28.212 --> 00:01:31.048
<v Tutor>Most of them die within about a week, uraemic.

23
00:01:31.048 --> 00:01:35.040
<v Tutor>At post-mortem the kidney tubules are full of brown pigmented casts.

24
00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:39.496
<v Tutor>A young physician at Hammersmith, Eric Bywaters, noticed the pattern.

25
00:01:39.496 --> 00:01:48.607
<v Tutor>With Desmond Beall he published four of these cases in the British Medical Journal in March 1941, under the title "Crush injuries with impairment of renal function".

26
00:01:48.607 --> 00:01:54.243
<v Tutor>The similarity to blackwater fever, where haemoglobin blocks the tubules, pointed toward a pigment.

27
00:01:54.243 --> 00:01:58.298
<v Tutor>That pigment turned out to be myoglobin, leaking from damaged muscle.

28
00:01:58.298 --> 00:02:05.040
<v Tutor>Bywaters later found that German doctors had described the same thing in the First World War, and it had been forgotten.

29
00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:09.014
<v Tutor>Two lessons from that story are still exam points today.

30
00:02:09.014 --> 00:02:14.096
<v Tutor>The first is that the danger comes after the rescue, when the muscle is reperfused.

31
00:02:14.096 --> 00:02:20.071
<v Tutor>Potassium, phosphate, myoglobin and acid flood out of the muscle, and litres of fluid move into it.

32
00:02:20.071 --> 00:02:25.433
<v Tutor>The second is that the kidney injury is largely preventable with early, generous volume.

33
00:02:25.433 --> 00:02:28.560
<v Tutor>That's why the College answer leads with volume.

34
00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:31.876
<v Tutor>This is the 2020 first sitting, question one.

35
00:02:31.876 --> 00:02:37.597
<v Tutor>With regards to rhabdomyolysis in the ICU patient: a, list five causes, twenty percent of marks.

36
00:02:37.597 --> 00:02:45.629
<v Tutor>b, what are the important features in the history and clinical examination, and what specific laboratory investigations would you request?

37
00:02:45.629 --> 00:02:47.389
<v Tutor>Thirty percent of marks.

38
00:02:47.389 --> 00:02:49.260
<v Tutor>c, outline the management.

39
00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:50.965
<v Tutor>Fifty percent of marks.

40
00:02:50.965 --> 00:02:56.852
<v Tutor>Seventy-eight percent of candidates passed it, so it's a question you're expected to score well on.

41
00:02:56.852 --> 00:03:04.279
<v Tutor>Rhabdomyolysis has turned up five times in the question bank we're working from, most recently twice in the 2025 first sitting.

42
00:03:04.279 --> 00:03:10.000
<v Tutor>You scored five on this on the ninth of November, and you used under half of the available time.

43
00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:13.605
<v Tutor>Here's the answer that earns the marks.

44
00:03:13.605 --> 00:03:19.742
<v Tutor>For part a, give one cause per heading, because the College gave half a mark each.

45
00:03:19.742 --> 00:03:21.447
<v Tutor>Trauma and crush.

46
00:03:21.447 --> 00:03:23.492
<v Tutor>Exertion and seizures.

47
00:03:23.492 --> 00:03:29.561
<v Tutor>Drugs, such as statins and antipsychotics through neuroleptic malignant syndrome.

48
00:03:29.561 --> 00:03:32.970
<v Tutor>Toxins, such as alcohol, cocaine and MDMA.

49
00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:38.494
<v Tutor>Temperature, meaning heat stroke, malignant hyperthermia, or hypothermia.

50
00:03:38.494 --> 00:03:42.721
<v Tutor>Infection, such as influenza or necrotising fasciitis.

51
00:03:42.721 --> 00:03:48.040
<v Tutor>And metabolic causes, like severe hypokalaemia or hyperosmolar states.

52
00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:50.656
<v Tutor>Part b is where your marks went.

53
00:03:50.656 --> 00:03:58.191
<v Tutor>The question asks for specific laboratory investigations, and the examiners noted that some candidates offered CT scans instead.

54
00:03:58.191 --> 00:03:59.410
<v Tutor>Read the verb.

55
00:03:59.410 --> 00:04:04.895
<v Tutor>Give creatine kinase, with a risk rising steeply above about five thousand units per litre.

56
00:04:04.895 --> 00:04:11.377
<v Tutor>Creatinine, and note that the urea to creatinine ratio may be low because creatinine is released from muscle.

57
00:04:11.377 --> 00:04:13.427
<v Tutor>Then the dangerous chemistry.

58
00:04:13.427 --> 00:04:14.425
<v Tutor>Potassium.

59
00:04:14.425 --> 00:04:15.422
<v Tutor>Phosphate.

60
00:04:15.422 --> 00:04:17.417
<v Tutor>Calcium, which is low early.

61
00:04:17.417 --> 00:04:18.192
<v Tutor>Urate.

62
00:04:18.192 --> 00:04:21.018
<v Tutor>A blood gas with lactate and the anion gap.

63
00:04:21.018 --> 00:04:25.118
<v Tutor>Urine dipstick positive for blood with no red cells on microscopy.

64
00:04:25.118 --> 00:04:30.270
<v Tutor>Then the cause-directed tests: a drug screen, alcohol, glucose, and thyroid function.

65
00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:35.478
<v Tutor>Clinically, ask about trauma, immobility, seizures, drugs, muscle pain and dark urine.

66
00:04:35.478 --> 00:04:39.800
<v Tutor>Examine every compartment, the temperature, and the peripheral pulses.

67
00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:41.902
<v Tutor>Part c, half the marks.

68
00:04:41.902 --> 00:04:44.428
<v Tutor>First, stop the ongoing muscle injury.

69
00:04:44.428 --> 00:04:48.602
<v Tutor>Treat the cause, stop the drug, control agitation, cool the patient.

70
00:04:48.602 --> 00:04:52.610
<v Tutor>Second, find limb-threatening and life-threatening complications.

71
00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:56.893
<v Tutor>Compartment syndrome, with compartment pressures and early fasciotomy.

72
00:04:56.893 --> 00:05:00.353
<v Tutor>Third, treat the electrolytes, hyperkalaemia above all.

73
00:05:00.353 --> 00:05:06.118
<v Tutor>Fourth, early, aggressive volume resuscitation with crystalloid, titrated to a high urine output.

74
00:05:06.118 --> 00:05:11.610
<v Tutor>The College accepted saline in 2020, and a balanced crystalloid is equally defensible today.

75
00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:18.144
<v Tutor>Bicarbonate and mannitol are described in the literature, but the College specifically said they earn no marks.

76
00:05:18.144 --> 00:05:22.043
<v Tutor>And fifth, renal replacement therapy for the usual indications.

77
00:05:22.043 --> 00:05:23.800
<v Tutor>Not to remove myoglobin.

78
00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:25.999
<v Tutor>Now let's make it harder.

79
00:05:25.999 --> 00:05:32.650
<v Tutor>A twenty-four-year-old army recruit collapses near the end of a ten-kilometre march on a humid Queensland morning.

80
00:05:32.650 --> 00:05:34.612
<v Tutor>He's confused and combative.

81
00:05:34.612 --> 00:05:38.101
<v Tutor>His rectal temperature is forty-one point eight degrees.

82
00:05:38.101 --> 00:05:50.040
<v Tutor>Two hours later, after cooling, his creatine kinase is forty thousand units per litre, his potassium is seven point four millimoles per litre, and the ECG shows peaked T waves and a QRS that's starting to widen.

83
00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:57.317
<v Tutor>That's exertional heat stroke: a core temperature above forty degrees with central nervous system dysfunction after exertion.

84
00:05:57.317 --> 00:06:00.470
<v Tutor>The first rule is to cool first and transport second.

85
00:06:00.470 --> 00:06:09.363
<v Tutor>Cold water immersion is the fastest method, and the aim is to get the core temperature below thirty-nine degrees as fast as possible, ideally within thirty minutes.

86
00:06:09.363 --> 00:06:13.448
<v Tutor>The longer the brain and muscle sit above forty, the worse the outcome.

87
00:06:13.448 --> 00:06:17.481
<v Tutor>Antipyretics don't work, because the hypothalamic set point is normal.

88
00:06:17.481 --> 00:06:21.048
<v Tutor>Dantrolene has no proven role outside malignant hyperthermia.

89
00:06:21.048 --> 00:06:28.080
<v Tutor>Watch for disseminated intravascular coagulation, liver injury peaking on day two or three, and the rhabdomyolysis you'd expect.

90
00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:31.329
<v Tutor>Now the hyperkalaemia with a widening QRS.

91
00:06:31.329 --> 00:06:34.520
<v Tutor>This is the moment the question becomes cardiac.

92
00:06:34.520 --> 00:06:36.799
<v Tutor>Three numbers, each with a dose.

93
00:06:36.799 --> 00:06:38.280
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

94
00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:45.773
<v Tutor>First, stabilise the membrane.

95
00:06:45.773 --> 00:06:54.860
<v Tutor>Ten millilitres of ten percent calcium chloride, or thirty millilitres of ten percent calcium gluconate, through a good line, repeated until the QRS narrows.

96
00:06:54.860 --> 00:06:57.228
<v Tutor>Second, shift potassium into cells.

97
00:06:57.228 --> 00:07:08.187
<v Tutor>Ten units of short-acting insulin with fifty millilitres of fifty percent glucose, with glucose monitoring for the next four to six hours, plus nebulised salbutamol, ten to twenty milligrams.

98
00:07:08.187 --> 00:07:09.564
<v Tutor>Third, remove it.

99
00:07:09.564 --> 00:07:17.880
<v Tutor>In a rhabdomyolysis patient who's oliguric, that means early renal replacement therapy, because the muscle keeps releasing potassium for hours.

100
00:07:18.280 --> 00:07:21.781
<v Tutor>Here's the subtlety that separates a pass from a good pass.

101
00:07:21.781 --> 00:07:25.387
<v Tutor>In rhabdomyolysis you don't treat asymptomatic hypocalcaemia.

102
00:07:25.387 --> 00:07:31.709
<v Tutor>Calcium deposits in injured muscle, and as the muscle recovers it comes back out, causing rebound hypercalcaemia.

103
00:07:31.709 --> 00:07:36.569
<v Tutor>But when hyperkalaemia is threatening the heart, you give calcium without hesitation.

104
00:07:36.569 --> 00:07:42.160
<v Tutor>Say both halves of that sentence in an answer and the examiner knows you understand the physiology.

105
00:07:43.060 --> 00:07:44.331
<v Examiner>Let's push on.

106
00:07:44.331 --> 00:07:49.589
<v Examiner>Six hours later his right calf is tense, and he's grimacing when you move his foot.

107
00:07:49.589 --> 00:07:52.362
<v Examiner>He's sedated, so he can't tell you much.

108
00:07:52.362 --> 00:07:55.540
<v Examiner>Candidate, what's going on, and what do you do?

109
00:07:56.040 --> 00:07:58.220
<v Candidate>I'm worried about compartment syndrome.

110
00:07:58.220 --> 00:08:04.851
<v Candidate>The swollen muscle is inside a fixed fascial envelope, so the compartment pressure rises until it exceeds capillary perfusion pressure.

111
00:08:04.851 --> 00:08:08.654
<v Candidate>The clinical signs are pain out of proportion and pain on passive stretch.

112
00:08:08.654 --> 00:08:12.920
<v Candidate>In a sedated patient those are unreliable, so I'd measure the compartment pressures.

113
00:08:13.420 --> 00:08:14.500
<v Examiner>What numbers?

114
00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:18.795
<v Candidate>An absolute pressure above thirty millimetres of mercury is concerning.

115
00:08:18.795 --> 00:08:21.341
<v Candidate>The more useful number is the delta pressure.

116
00:08:21.341 --> 00:08:28.834
<v Candidate>That's the diastolic blood pressure minus the compartment pressure, and a value under thirty millimetres of mercury is an indication for fasciotomy.

117
00:08:28.834 --> 00:08:33.254
<v Candidate>Pulses are usually present until very late, so a palpable pulse doesn't reassure me.

118
00:08:33.254 --> 00:08:36.520
<v Candidate>I'd call the orthopaedic or vascular surgeons straight away.

119
00:08:37.020 --> 00:08:40.020
<v Examiner>And if his diastolic is low because he's shocked?

120
00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:43.894
<v Candidate>Then the delta pressure falls, even at the same compartment pressure.

121
00:08:43.894 --> 00:08:48.320
<v Candidate>So restoring the blood pressure is part of treating the limb, and I'd say that in the answer.

122
00:08:48.820 --> 00:08:49.440
<v Examiner>Good.

123
00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:50.250
<v Examiner>One more.

124
00:08:50.250 --> 00:08:54.780
<v Examiner>Why does the creatine kinase keep rising for a day or two after the insult has stopped?

125
00:08:55.280 --> 00:09:01.183
<v Candidate>Ongoing release from injured and reperfused muscle, and new necrosis if there's an untreated compartment syndrome.

126
00:09:01.183 --> 00:09:08.440
<v Candidate>A creatine kinase that doesn't start falling after about seventy-two hours should make me look for a missed compartment or a continuing cause.

127
00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:10.733
<v Examiner>That's a candidate who'll pass.

128
00:09:10.733 --> 00:09:12.940
<v Examiner>Notice that none of it needed a CT scan.

129
00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:15.418
<v Tutor>Picture this.

130
00:09:15.418 --> 00:09:22.655
<v Tutor>It's three in the morning and a fifty-eight-year-old woman on haemodialysis three times a week arrives from the emergency department.

131
00:09:22.655 --> 00:09:29.739
<v Tutor>She has a fever, a blood pressure of seventy-eight over forty, and a hot, red, tender left forearm over her arteriovenous fistula.

132
00:09:29.739 --> 00:09:32.049
<v Tutor>Her last dialysis was three days ago.

133
00:09:32.049 --> 00:09:35.180
<v Tutor>Her potassium is six point nine millimoles per litre.

134
00:09:35.580 --> 00:09:38.813
<v Tutor>This is the 2021 first sitting, question eight.

135
00:09:38.813 --> 00:09:48.805
<v Tutor>Outline the pathophysiological changes associated with end-stage kidney disease, dialysis dependent, that may impact on the management of critically ill patients.

136
00:09:48.805 --> 00:09:53.919
<v Tutor>That theme has been asked four times in the bank, in 2011, 2016, 2020 and 2021.

137
00:09:53.919 --> 00:09:57.152
<v Tutor>You scored five on it on the ninth of November.

138
00:09:57.152 --> 00:10:01.090
<v Tutor>Your pharmacology was good, but whole systems were missing.

139
00:10:01.090 --> 00:10:02.854
<v Tutor>The fix is structural.

140
00:10:02.854 --> 00:10:10.260
<v Tutor>This question rewards a system-by-system answer, and a table works well: system, change, and impact on ICU management.

141
00:10:10.660 --> 00:10:12.456
<v Tutor>Let's run through it.

142
00:10:12.456 --> 00:10:22.425
<v Tutor>Renal: oliguria or anuria, so every millilitre of fluid, every drug infusion and every gram of protein in the feed is prescribed against a dialysis plan.

143
00:10:22.425 --> 00:10:30.846
<v Tutor>Metabolic: hyperkalaemia, hyperphosphataemia and hypocalcaemia, a chronic metabolic acidosis, and secondary hyperparathyroidism.

144
00:10:30.846 --> 00:10:34.561
<v Tutor>So you restrict potassium and phosphate in the feed.

145
00:10:34.561 --> 00:10:44.468
<v Tutor>Cardiovascular: hypertension, left ventricular hypertrophy, accelerated atherosclerosis, uraemic pericarditis, and a low threshold for pulmonary oedema.

146
00:10:44.468 --> 00:10:49.112
<v Tutor>Neurological: dialysis disequilibrium, polyneuropathy and myopathy.

147
00:10:49.112 --> 00:10:53.199
<v Tutor>Haematological: anaemia, and uraemic platelet dysfunction.

148
00:10:53.199 --> 00:10:56.419
<v Tutor>GI: impaired motility, ulceration, bleeding.

149
00:10:56.419 --> 00:11:00.568
<v Tutor>Immune: a real increase in infection risk and fragile skin.

150
00:11:00.568 --> 00:11:04.654
<v Tutor>Pharmacology: reduced clearance of renally excreted drugs.

151
00:11:04.654 --> 00:11:08.060
<v Tutor>And access, which candidates forget every time.

152
00:11:08.460 --> 00:11:11.144
<v Tutor>Why do we protect the fistula so fiercely?

153
00:11:11.144 --> 00:11:12.540
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

154
00:11:17.940 --> 00:11:20.980
<v Tutor>Because it's her lifeline, and it's hard to replace.

155
00:11:20.980 --> 00:11:23.817
<v Tutor>No blood pressure cuffs or cannulas on that arm.

156
00:11:23.817 --> 00:11:31.265
<v Tutor>Avoid central lines in the veins draining it, especially the subclavian, because central venous stenosis can destroy the fistula's outflow.

157
00:11:31.265 --> 00:11:37.244
<v Tutor>And never use the fistula for continuous renal replacement therapy or slow low-efficiency dialysis in the ICU.

158
00:11:37.244 --> 00:11:44.591
<v Tutor>Needles stay in for hours in a drowsy, moving patient, and needle dislodgement from a high-flow fistula can exsanguinate them in minutes.

159
00:11:44.591 --> 00:11:49.100
<v Tutor>Put in a temporary dialysis catheter instead, ideally the right internal jugular.

160
00:11:49.500 --> 00:11:50.994
<v Tutor>Now the harder layer.

161
00:11:50.994 --> 00:11:53.827
<v Tutor>She's septic, almost certainly from the access.

162
00:11:53.827 --> 00:11:56.248
<v Tutor>What changes because she's on dialysis?

163
00:11:56.248 --> 00:11:57.587
<v Tutor>Antibiotics first.

164
00:11:57.587 --> 00:12:02.326
<v Tutor>Give a full loading dose, because the volume of distribution is unchanged or bigger.

165
00:12:02.326 --> 00:12:05.211
<v Tutor>It's only the maintenance doses that you reduce.

166
00:12:05.211 --> 00:12:12.320
<v Tutor>Vancomycin at a loading dose of about twenty-five milligrams per kilogram, then dosing guided by levels and timed around dialysis.

167
00:12:12.320 --> 00:12:17.780
<v Tutor>Remember that a clotted or infected fistula is a source, and the vascular surgeons need to see it.

168
00:12:18.180 --> 00:12:19.891
<v Tutor>Second, the potassium.

169
00:12:19.891 --> 00:12:23.255
<v Tutor>Six point nine with a three-day gap is predictable.

170
00:12:23.255 --> 00:12:28.956
<v Tutor>In a stable patient, intermittent haemodialysis removes potassium faster than anything else.

171
00:12:28.956 --> 00:12:33.062
<v Tutor>In septic shock, continuous therapy is usually better tolerated.

172
00:12:33.062 --> 00:12:37.053
<v Tutor>Either way, temporise first with calcium, insulin and glucose.

173
00:12:37.053 --> 00:12:43.553
<v Tutor>And if you intubate her, remember that suxamethonium raises potassium by about half a millimole per litre.

174
00:12:43.553 --> 00:12:46.860
<v Tutor>At six point nine, rocuronium is the safer choice.

175
00:12:47.260 --> 00:12:48.697
<v Tutor>Third, the fluids.

176
00:12:48.697 --> 00:12:51.683
<v Tutor>Here's where a landmark trial earns its place.

177
00:12:51.683 --> 00:12:57.045
<v Tutor>Many of us were taught that saline is safer in renal failure because it has no potassium.

178
00:12:57.045 --> 00:12:58.427
<v Tutor>That's backwards.

179
00:12:58.427 --> 00:13:03.182
<v Tutor>Saline causes a hyperchloraemic acidosis, which shifts potassium out of cells.

180
00:13:03.182 --> 00:13:10.700
<v Tutor>Balanced solutions contain a little potassium, around five millimoles per litre in Plasma-Lyte, but they don't drive that shift.

181
00:13:11.100 --> 00:13:20.368
<v Tutor>The BaSICS trial, published by Zampieri and colleagues in JAMA in 2021, randomised eleven thousand and fifty-two ICU patients across seventy-five Brazilian units.

182
00:13:20.368 --> 00:13:22.821
<v Tutor>It was a two by two factorial design.

183
00:13:22.821 --> 00:13:34.107
<v Tutor>Plasma-Lyte was compared with zero point nine percent saline, and a slow bolus rate of three hundred and thirty-three millilitres an hour was compared with a fast one of nine hundred and ninety-nine.

184
00:13:34.107 --> 00:13:41.576
<v Tutor>Ninety-day mortality was twenty-six point four percent with the balanced solution and twenty-seven point two percent with saline.

185
00:13:41.576 --> 00:13:48.772
<v Tutor>That's a hazard ratio of zero point nine seven, with a confidence interval from zero point nine zero to one point zero five.

186
00:13:48.772 --> 00:13:49.971
<v Tutor>No difference.

187
00:13:49.971 --> 00:13:52.806
<v Tutor>The infusion rate made no difference either.

188
00:13:52.806 --> 00:14:02.074
<v Tutor>The worrying signal was in traumatic brain injury, where mortality was higher with the balanced fluid: thirty-one point three versus twenty-one point one percent.

189
00:14:02.074 --> 00:14:07.254
<v Tutor>Plasma-Lyte is mildly hypotonic, so that fits the physiology, though it was a subgroup.

190
00:14:07.254 --> 00:14:16.140
<v Tutor>The criticisms: a low-acuity population, about half of them elective surgical, modest fluid volumes, and a small difference in chloride between the groups.

191
00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:31.256
<v Tutor>Here's how to quote it in an SAQ: "BaSICS, eleven thousand patients, showed no mortality difference between balanced crystalloid and saline, with a possible harm signal for balanced fluid in traumatic brain injury." For our dialysis patient, either fluid is acceptable.

192
00:14:31.256 --> 00:14:37.080
<v Tutor>Give modest boluses, judge each one, and don't let fear of potassium push you into litres of saline.

193
00:14:37.480 --> 00:14:41.915
<v Tutor>A brief detour on where dialysis came from, because it's a remarkable story.

194
00:14:41.915 --> 00:14:49.518
<v Tutor>In the early 1940s, in the occupied Netherlands, a young physician called Willem Kolff built an artificial kidney in the town of Kampen.

195
00:14:49.518 --> 00:14:56.382
<v Tutor>He wrapped cellophane sausage casing around a wooden drum that rotated through a bath of dialysis fluid in an enamel tank.

196
00:14:56.382 --> 00:14:58.494
<v Tutor>Most of his early patients died.

197
00:14:58.494 --> 00:15:03.985
<v Tutor>Then in September 1945 a sixty-seven-year-old woman in uraemic coma was dialysed for many hours.

198
00:15:03.985 --> 00:15:05.727
<v Tutor>She woke up and survived.

199
00:15:05.727 --> 00:15:12.749
<v Tutor>Kolff gave his machines and blueprints away after the war, and they seeded dialysis programmes on both sides of the Atlantic.

200
00:15:12.749 --> 00:15:17.343
<v Tutor>He later contributed to the intra-aortic balloon pump and the artificial heart.

201
00:15:17.343 --> 00:15:20.880
<v Tutor>Every dialysis catheter you put in descends from that drum.

202
00:15:21.280 --> 00:15:22.750
<v Tutor>One more harder twist.

203
00:15:22.750 --> 00:15:30.199
<v Tutor>Your dialysis patient needs an emergency laparotomy and bleeds from every puncture site, with a normal platelet count and normal clotting times.

204
00:15:30.199 --> 00:15:32.355
<v Tutor>That's uraemic platelet dysfunction.

205
00:15:32.355 --> 00:15:33.482
<v Tutor>Dialysis helps.

206
00:15:33.482 --> 00:15:37.647
<v Tutor>Correct the anaemia, because red cells push platelets out to the vessel wall.

207
00:15:37.647 --> 00:15:41.862
<v Tutor>And give desmopressin, zero point three micrograms per kilogram intravenously.

208
00:15:41.862 --> 00:15:43.038
<v Tutor>Notice the unit.

209
00:15:43.038 --> 00:15:43.969
<v Tutor>Micrograms.

210
00:15:43.969 --> 00:15:45.880
<v Tutor>We're about to need that again.

211
00:15:46.780 --> 00:15:48.391
<v Examiner>Before we leave her.

212
00:15:48.391 --> 00:15:50.521
<v Examiner>Different patient, same unit.

213
00:15:50.521 --> 00:15:58.233
<v Examiner>A forty-year-old man presents with a urea of fifty-five millimoles per litre, a GCS of thirteen, and he's never been dialysed.

214
00:15:58.233 --> 00:16:04.679
<v Examiner>Candidate, the registrar wants to run a four-hour intermittent haemodialysis session at full blood flow.

215
00:16:04.679 --> 00:16:06.060
<v Examiner>What's the risk?

216
00:16:06.560 --> 00:16:08.644
<v Candidate>Dialysis disequilibrium syndrome.

217
00:16:08.644 --> 00:16:12.405
<v Candidate>Urea is removed from the blood faster than it can leave the brain.

218
00:16:12.405 --> 00:16:17.334
<v Candidate>That leaves an osmotic gradient, and water moves into the brain, causing cerebral oedema.

219
00:16:17.334 --> 00:16:21.400
<v Candidate>It ranges from headache and nausea through to seizures, coma, and death.

220
00:16:21.900 --> 00:16:23.140
<v Examiner>Who's at risk?

221
00:16:23.640 --> 00:16:25.859
<v Candidate>New patients with a very high urea.

222
00:16:25.859 --> 00:16:30.607
<v Candidate>Patients with existing brain injury, raised intracranial pressure, or hyponatraemia.

223
00:16:30.607 --> 00:16:32.620
<v Candidate>The very young and the elderly.

224
00:16:32.620 --> 00:16:35.200
<v Candidate>And anyone with severe metabolic acidosis.

225
00:16:35.700 --> 00:16:37.060
<v Examiner>How do you prevent it?

226
00:16:37.560 --> 00:16:39.473
<v Candidate>Make the first session gentle.

227
00:16:39.473 --> 00:16:47.732
<v Candidate>Use a short session with a lower blood flow and a smaller surface area filter, aiming to lower the urea by no more than about thirty percent the first time.

228
00:16:47.732 --> 00:16:49.192
<v Candidate>Then repeat it daily.

229
00:16:49.192 --> 00:16:53.321
<v Candidate>Or use continuous renal replacement therapy, which lowers the urea slowly.

230
00:16:53.321 --> 00:17:00.320
<v Candidate>In a patient with brain injury or raised intracranial pressure, I'd choose continuous therapy, and I'd keep the sodium high-normal.

231
00:17:00.820 --> 00:17:01.492
<v Examiner>Good.

232
00:17:01.492 --> 00:17:05.420
<v Examiner>That's worth a mark in any question that asks about modality choice.

233
00:17:06.820 --> 00:17:10.461
<v Tutor>This is the 2018 first sitting, question five point two.

234
00:17:10.461 --> 00:17:17.686
<v Tutor>A 47-year-old, previously well, 70 kilogram male was admitted to the Emergency Department with agitation and confusion.

235
00:17:17.686 --> 00:17:23.944
<v Tutor>Following catheterisation, his urine output in the next few hours was 300 to 350 millilitres per hour.

236
00:17:23.944 --> 00:17:27.016
<v Tutor>The results of his blood tests are as follows.

237
00:17:27.016 --> 00:17:30.657
<v Tutor>Sodium, one hundred and sixty-nine millimoles per litre.

238
00:17:30.657 --> 00:17:33.786
<v Tutor>Potassium, four point six millimoles per litre.

239
00:17:33.786 --> 00:17:37.199
<v Tutor>Chloride, one hundred and five millimoles per litre.

240
00:17:37.199 --> 00:17:40.214
<v Tutor>Bicarbonate, twenty-six millimoles per litre.

241
00:17:40.214 --> 00:17:43.172
<v Tutor>Glucose, six point six millimoles per litre.

242
00:17:43.172 --> 00:17:46.017
<v Tutor>Urea, nine point six millimoles per litre.

243
00:17:46.017 --> 00:17:49.715
<v Tutor>Creatinine, one hundred and fifteen micromoles per litre.

244
00:17:49.715 --> 00:17:53.299
<v Tutor>Magnesium, zero point eight eight millimoles per litre.

245
00:17:53.299 --> 00:17:55.916
<v Tutor>Albumin, twenty-eight grams per litre.

246
00:17:55.916 --> 00:17:58.476
<v Tutor>Protein, fifty-eight grams per litre.

247
00:17:58.476 --> 00:18:02.743
<v Tutor>Plasma osmolality, three hundred and sixty millimoles per kilogram.

248
00:18:02.743 --> 00:18:05.985
<v Tutor>Urine specific gravity, one point zero zero five.

249
00:18:05.985 --> 00:18:08.545
<v Tutor>a, what is the most likely diagnosis?

250
00:18:08.545 --> 00:18:10.195
<v Tutor>Ten percent of marks.

251
00:18:10.195 --> 00:18:13.040
<v Tutor>b, how would you manage the hypernatremia?

252
00:18:13.040 --> 00:18:14.860
<v Tutor>Thirty percent of marks.

253
00:18:15.260 --> 00:18:21.676
<v Tutor>The examiners wrote that management of diabetes insipidus was handled poorly, and that some fluid regimens were dangerous.

254
00:18:21.676 --> 00:18:25.624
<v Tutor>You first met this question on the fifteenth of October and scored five.

255
00:18:25.624 --> 00:18:30.657
<v Tutor>When you came back to it on the tenth of November you scored six, so it's technically retired.

256
00:18:30.657 --> 00:18:34.754
<v Tutor>But the desmopressin dose was written in milligrams rather than micrograms.

257
00:18:34.754 --> 00:18:36.579
<v Tutor>That's a thousand-fold error.

258
00:18:36.579 --> 00:18:38.405
<v Tutor>In the exam it's a lost mark.

259
00:18:38.405 --> 00:18:40.330
<v Tutor>On the ward it's a catastrophe.

260
00:18:40.330 --> 00:18:41.860
<v Tutor>So it comes back today.

261
00:18:42.260 --> 00:18:44.565
<v Tutor>Diagnosis: diabetes insipidus.

262
00:18:44.565 --> 00:18:53.180
<v Tutor>Severe hypernatraemia, high plasma osmolality, and a large volume of dilute urine with a specific gravity of one point zero zero five.

263
00:18:53.180 --> 00:18:56.274
<v Tutor>The answer that scores then has five moves.

264
00:18:56.274 --> 00:19:00.763
<v Tutor>One, assess volume and cardiac status, because he may also be dry.

265
00:19:00.763 --> 00:19:04.707
<v Tutor>Two, calculate the water deficit and choose a time frame.

266
00:19:04.707 --> 00:19:11.562
<v Tutor>Three, give free water as five percent dextrose or enteral water, and match the ongoing urine losses too.

267
00:19:11.562 --> 00:19:14.838
<v Tutor>Four, decide central or nephrogenic and treat.

268
00:19:14.838 --> 00:19:18.660
<v Tutor>Five, check the sodium every one to two hours at first.

269
00:19:19.060 --> 00:19:20.699
<v Tutor>Let's do the arithmetic.

270
00:19:20.699 --> 00:19:22.542
<v Tutor>He weighs seventy kilograms.

271
00:19:22.542 --> 00:19:24.488
<v Tutor>What's his free water deficit?

272
00:19:24.488 --> 00:19:25.820
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

273
00:19:31.220 --> 00:19:35.313
<v Tutor>Total body water is sixty percent of seventy, which is forty-two litres.

274
00:19:35.313 --> 00:19:40.991
<v Tutor>The deficit is forty-two multiplied by the ratio of the current sodium to the target sodium, minus one.

275
00:19:40.991 --> 00:19:44.317
<v Tutor>One sixty-nine over one forty is about one point two one.

276
00:19:44.317 --> 00:19:49.637
<v Tutor>So the deficit is forty-two times zero point two one, which is roughly eight point seven litres.

277
00:19:49.637 --> 00:19:54.037
<v Tutor>And that's on top of a urine output of over three hundred millilitres an hour.

278
00:19:54.037 --> 00:19:58.232
<v Tutor>That's more than seven litres a day, which you have to replace as it goes.

279
00:19:58.232 --> 00:20:01.660
<v Tutor>Unless you control the urine output, you'll never catch up.

280
00:20:02.060 --> 00:20:03.022
<v Tutor>How fast?

281
00:20:03.022 --> 00:20:10.834
<v Tutor>If the hypernatraemia is clearly acute, under forty-eight hours, you can lower it faster, around one millimole per litre per hour.

282
00:20:10.834 --> 00:20:23.174
<v Tutor>If it's chronic or the duration is unknown, aim for no more than ten to twelve millimoles per litre in twenty-four hours, because the brain has made idiogenic osmoles and rapid correction risks cerebral oedema.

283
00:20:23.174 --> 00:20:33.420
<v Tutor>One more useful number: each litre of five percent dextrose will lower his sodium by about one sixty-nine divided by forty-three, which is roughly four millimoles per litre.

284
00:20:33.820 --> 00:20:35.623
<v Tutor>Then the specific therapy.

285
00:20:35.623 --> 00:20:42.145
<v Tutor>Desmopressin, one to two micrograms intravenously or subcutaneously, repeated according to urine output and sodium.

286
00:20:42.145 --> 00:20:43.153
<v Tutor>Micrograms.

287
00:20:43.153 --> 00:20:47.925
<v Tutor>If the urine concentrates and the output falls, the diabetes insipidus is central.

288
00:20:47.925 --> 00:20:53.546
<v Tutor>If it doesn't, think nephrogenic, and in a previously well forty-seven-year-old ask about lithium.

289
00:20:53.546 --> 00:20:59.060
<v Tutor>Then the management changes: stop the lithium, check the level, and use a thiazide or amiloride.

290
00:20:59.060 --> 00:21:04.840
<v Tutor>Amiloride blocks the epithelial sodium channel that lithium enters the collecting duct cells through.

291
00:21:04.840 --> 00:21:10.620
<v Tutor>Lithium-induced nephrogenic diabetes insipidus was the focus of a question in the 2026 first sitting.

292
00:21:11.020 --> 00:21:16.984
<v Tutor>In the ICU, central diabetes insipidus has a short list of causes, and you should be able to recite it.

293
00:21:16.984 --> 00:21:19.025
<v Tutor>Severe traumatic brain injury.

294
00:21:19.025 --> 00:21:20.798
<v Tutor>Subarachnoid haemorrhage.

295
00:21:20.798 --> 00:21:23.484
<v Tutor>Hypoxic brain injury after cardiac arrest.

296
00:21:23.484 --> 00:21:25.741
<v Tutor>Pituitary or hypothalamic surgery.

297
00:21:25.741 --> 00:21:35.196
<v Tutor>And brain death, where diabetes insipidus is so common that its sudden appearance in a deteriorating neurosurgical patient should make you check the brainstem reflexes.

298
00:21:35.196 --> 00:21:49.380
<v Tutor>In the organ donor, the targets are a sodium below about one hundred and fifty-five millimoles per litre and a urine output brought back to between half a millilitre and three millilitres per kilogram per hour, using desmopressin or a vasopressin infusion.

299
00:21:49.780 --> 00:21:53.201
<v Tutor>After pituitary surgery, watch for the triphasic response.

300
00:21:53.201 --> 00:21:59.681
<v Tutor>First comes polyuria from diabetes insipidus over the first few days, as the damaged neurones stop releasing hormone.

301
00:21:59.681 --> 00:22:10.204
<v Tutor>Then, for about a week, there's an antidiuretic phase as the dying neurones release their stored hormone, and the patient can become dangerously hyponatraemic if you're still giving desmopressin.

302
00:22:10.204 --> 00:22:13.158
<v Tutor>Finally, permanent diabetes insipidus can follow.

303
00:22:13.158 --> 00:22:17.150
<v Tutor>So the drug that rescued them on day two can drown them on day seven.

304
00:22:17.150 --> 00:22:25.340
<v Tutor>That's why you give desmopressin as single doses guided by the urine output and sodium, rather than as a fixed regular prescription in the first week.

305
00:22:25.740 --> 00:22:29.169
<v Tutor>Now the twist, and it links to one of today's Fresh cards.

306
00:22:29.169 --> 00:22:37.637
<v Tutor>Same man, but he's had a prolonged cardiac arrest from the agitation, and he now has an acute kidney injury and needs continuous renal replacement therapy.

307
00:22:37.637 --> 00:22:40.495
<v Tutor>His sodium is still one hundred and sixty-nine.

308
00:22:40.495 --> 00:22:45.846
<v Tutor>Standard replacement fluid contains about one hundred and forty millimoles per litre of sodium.

309
00:22:45.846 --> 00:22:48.340
<v Tutor>What happens when you start the circuit?

310
00:22:49.240 --> 00:22:50.527
<v Examiner>I'll take that one.

311
00:22:50.527 --> 00:22:52.720
<v Examiner>Candidate, what happens to his sodium?

312
00:22:53.220 --> 00:22:56.063
<v Candidate>The circuit equilibrates plasma with the fluid.

313
00:22:56.063 --> 00:23:03.765
<v Candidate>With a normal effluent dose, his sodium will fall toward one forty quite quickly, potentially by well over ten millimoles per litre in a day.

314
00:23:03.765 --> 00:23:06.660
<v Candidate>That's the overcorrection we're trying to avoid.

315
00:23:07.160 --> 00:23:08.760
<v Examiner>So what do you do?

316
00:23:09.260 --> 00:23:11.623
<v Candidate>I'd set the fluid sodium close to his.

317
00:23:11.623 --> 00:23:19.946
<v Candidate>I'd add hypertonic saline to the bags so the sodium starts within about eight to ten millimoles per litre of his plasma level, then step it down each day.

318
00:23:19.946 --> 00:23:22.566
<v Candidate>I'd measure sodium every four to six hours.

319
00:23:22.566 --> 00:23:25.340
<v Candidate>I could also start with a lower effluent dose.

320
00:23:25.840 --> 00:23:26.533
<v Examiner>Good.

321
00:23:26.533 --> 00:23:30.000
<v Examiner>And the mirror image, a sodium of one hundred and twelve?

322
00:23:30.500 --> 00:23:32.538
<v Candidate>The same principle in reverse.

323
00:23:32.538 --> 00:23:38.973
<v Candidate>Lower the fluid sodium, for example by adding sterile water to the bags, or run five percent dextrose alongside.

324
00:23:38.973 --> 00:23:45.140
<v Candidate>That stops the circuit correcting it by twenty-eight millimoles in a day and causing osmotic demyelination.

325
00:23:45.640 --> 00:23:46.905
<v Examiner>That's the whole concept.

326
00:23:46.905 --> 00:23:49.283
<v Examiner>The circuit doesn't care about your correction limits.

327
00:23:49.283 --> 00:23:51.200
<v Examiner>You have to programme them into the fluid.

328
00:23:52.100 --> 00:23:58.902
<v Tutor>And a reminder from the hyponatraemia question you met in October, the 2011 second sitting, question five.

329
00:23:58.902 --> 00:24:06.301
<v Tutor>When you restore volume in hypovolaemic hyponatraemia, the baroreceptor drive for antidiuretic hormone switches off.

330
00:24:06.301 --> 00:24:10.299
<v Tutor>The kidney then dumps free water, and the sodium shoots up.

331
00:24:10.299 --> 00:24:14.357
<v Tutor>Watch the urine output and check the sodium every two hours.

332
00:24:14.357 --> 00:24:21.875
<v Tutor>Alcohol, malnutrition, hypokalaemia and elderly women on thiazides are the classic osmotic demyelination risk factors.

333
00:24:21.875 --> 00:24:24.978
<v Tutor>The same physiology runs in both directions.

334
00:24:24.978 --> 00:24:28.260
<v Tutor>Water moves fast, and the brain pays for speed.

335
00:24:29.660 --> 00:24:32.682
<v Tutor>This is the 2016 first sitting, question six.

336
00:24:32.682 --> 00:24:36.217
<v Tutor>The same question was used in the 2015 second sitting.

337
00:24:36.217 --> 00:24:42.318
<v Tutor>A 76-year-old female is admitted to the ICU following elective aortic and mitral valve replacement.

338
00:24:42.318 --> 00:24:47.678
<v Tutor>Transoesophageal echo at the end of surgery showed an ejection fraction of 20 percent.

339
00:24:47.678 --> 00:24:51.384
<v Tutor>Her preoperative creatinine was 340 micromoles per litre.

340
00:24:51.384 --> 00:24:53.779
<v Tutor>Total bypass time was 240 minutes.

341
00:24:53.779 --> 00:24:57.428
<v Tutor>On arrival in ICU the patient has the following indices.

342
00:24:57.428 --> 00:25:00.222
<v Tutor>Temperature, thirty-five degrees Celsius.

343
00:25:00.222 --> 00:25:03.016
<v Tutor>Atrial pacing, AAI, at eighty per minute.

344
00:25:03.016 --> 00:25:07.805
<v Tutor>Systemic blood pressure, eighty-five over fifty-five millimetres of mercury.

345
00:25:07.805 --> 00:25:12.138
<v Tutor>Pulmonary artery pressure, sixty over thirty millimetres of mercury.

346
00:25:12.138 --> 00:25:16.301
<v Tutor>Cardiac index, one point five litres per minute per square metre.

347
00:25:16.301 --> 00:25:22.687
<v Tutor>Systemic vascular resistance, one thousand seven hundred dynes seconds per centimetre to the minus five.

348
00:25:22.687 --> 00:25:26.564
<v Tutor>Pulmonary artery wedge pressure, ten millimetres of mercury.

349
00:25:26.564 --> 00:25:30.099
<v Tutor>Central venous pressure, eight millimetres of mercury.

350
00:25:30.099 --> 00:25:33.292
<v Tutor>Adrenaline infusion, four micrograms per minute.

351
00:25:33.292 --> 00:25:38.937
<v Tutor>a, list the specific clinical and haemodynamic issues for this patient on admission to ICU.

352
00:25:38.937 --> 00:25:40.705
<v Tutor>Forty percent of marks.

353
00:25:40.705 --> 00:25:43.612
<v Tutor>b, outline your management of these issues.

354
00:25:43.612 --> 00:25:45.380
<v Tutor>Sixty percent of marks.

355
00:25:45.780 --> 00:25:51.968
<v Tutor>Ninety-five percent of candidates passed it, and the examiners' main complaint was superficial, generic management plans.

356
00:25:51.968 --> 00:25:54.846
<v Tutor>You scored four and a half on the tenth of November.

357
00:25:54.846 --> 00:25:57.100
<v Tutor>The core error was reading the numbers.

358
00:25:57.500 --> 00:25:59.100
<v Tutor>So, the numbers.

359
00:25:59.100 --> 00:26:06.233
<v Tutor>A cardiac index of one point five, a wedge of ten, a CVP of eight, and an SVR of seventeen hundred.

360
00:26:06.233 --> 00:26:08.767
<v Tutor>What's the haemodynamic state?

361
00:26:08.767 --> 00:26:10.500
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

362
00:26:15.900 --> 00:26:19.996
<v Tutor>A low output state with low-normal filling pressures and a high afterload.

363
00:26:19.996 --> 00:26:25.790
<v Tutor>The wedge of ten after a double valve replacement, in a stiff, hypertrophied ventricle, is probably too low.

364
00:26:25.790 --> 00:26:27.688
<v Tutor>She's likely fluid responsive.

365
00:26:27.688 --> 00:26:30.635
<v Tutor>The SVR is high, not low, so this isn't vasoplegia.

366
00:26:30.635 --> 00:26:38.027
<v Tutor>Reaching for noradrenaline would raise the afterload on a ventricle with an ejection fraction of twenty percent and drop the output further.

367
00:26:38.027 --> 00:26:44.820
<v Tutor>And a mean arterial pressure of sixty-five is probably not enough for a woman whose kidneys were already failing before surgery.

368
00:26:45.220 --> 00:26:48.194
<v Tutor>Now the issues list, which earns forty percent.

369
00:26:48.194 --> 00:26:52.520
<v Tutor>An elderly woman after a double valve operation with a long bypass time.

370
00:26:52.520 --> 00:26:54.953
<v Tutor>Pre-existing severe renal impairment.

371
00:26:54.953 --> 00:26:56.035
<v Tutor>Hypotension.

372
00:26:56.035 --> 00:26:59.441
<v Tutor>A low output state with poor left ventricular function.

373
00:26:59.441 --> 00:27:01.064
<v Tutor>Probable hypovolaemia.

374
00:27:01.064 --> 00:27:02.307
<v Tutor>High afterload.

375
00:27:02.307 --> 00:27:04.470
<v Tutor>Moderate pulmonary hypertension.

376
00:27:04.470 --> 00:27:05.552
<v Tutor>Hypothermia.

377
00:27:05.552 --> 00:27:11.500
<v Tutor>And, implicitly, the risks of bleeding and coagulopathy after two hundred and forty minutes on bypass.

378
00:27:11.900 --> 00:27:13.731
<v Tutor>Management, sixty percent.

379
00:27:13.731 --> 00:27:17.718
<v Tutor>Rewarm her, and give judicious fluid as she warms and vasodilates.

380
00:27:17.718 --> 00:27:26.767
<v Tutor>Get a bedside echo, transoesophageal if the windows are poor, looking for tamponade, prosthetic valve function, left and right ventricular filling and function.

381
00:27:26.767 --> 00:27:33.070
<v Tutor>Titrate the adrenaline to a cardiac index above two point two, and add an inodilator if the output stays low.

382
00:27:33.070 --> 00:27:41.850
<v Tutor>That might be milrinone, at around zero point three seven five to zero point seven five micrograms per kilogram per minute, or levosimendan, or dobutamine.

383
00:27:41.850 --> 00:27:46.644
<v Tutor>If she's still in low output despite that, consider an intra-aortic balloon pump.

384
00:27:46.644 --> 00:27:49.337
<v Tutor>Use AV sequential pacing at around ninety.

385
00:27:49.337 --> 00:27:53.808
<v Tutor>Correct coagulopathy and keep the haemoglobin above eighty grams per litre.

386
00:27:53.808 --> 00:27:56.448
<v Tutor>Plan early for renal replacement therapy.

387
00:27:56.448 --> 00:28:00.380
<v Tutor>And consider inhaled nitric oxide for the pulmonary hypertension.

388
00:28:00.780 --> 00:28:02.343
<v Tutor>Now let's go further.

389
00:28:02.343 --> 00:28:05.146
<v Tutor>Calculate her pulmonary vascular resistance.

390
00:28:05.146 --> 00:28:10.860
<v Tutor>The mean pulmonary artery pressure is sixty plus two times thirty, all over three, which is forty.

391
00:28:10.860 --> 00:28:15.333
<v Tutor>The transpulmonary gradient is forty minus a wedge of ten, which is thirty.

392
00:28:15.333 --> 00:28:26.060
<v Tutor>Her cardiac output is about two and a half litres a minute, because a cardiac index of one point five multiplied by a body surface area of about one point seven is around two point five five.

393
00:28:26.060 --> 00:28:34.469
<v Tutor>Thirty divided by two point five five is nearly twelve Wood units, or roughly nine hundred and forty dynes seconds per centimetre to the minus five.

394
00:28:34.469 --> 00:28:42.500
<v Tutor>That's severe pre-capillary pulmonary hypertension, probably from long-standing mitral disease, and it's a right ventricle under real strain.

395
00:28:43.400 --> 00:28:45.645
<v Examiner>Two hours later the nurse calls you.

396
00:28:45.645 --> 00:28:48.248
<v Examiner>The CVP has climbed from eight to eighteen.

397
00:28:48.248 --> 00:28:49.677
<v Examiner>The wedge is twelve.

398
00:28:49.677 --> 00:28:51.922
<v Examiner>The cardiac index is one point four.

399
00:28:51.922 --> 00:28:54.014
<v Examiner>The chest drains have gone quiet.

400
00:28:54.014 --> 00:28:55.800
<v Examiner>What are you worried about?

401
00:28:56.300 --> 00:28:57.329
<v Candidate>Two things.

402
00:28:57.329 --> 00:29:03.396
<v Candidate>Tamponade, because the drains stopping while the CVP rises is a classic sign of clot in the pericardium.

403
00:29:03.396 --> 00:29:10.330
<v Candidate>And acute right ventricular failure, because she has severe pulmonary hypertension and she's just had a long bypass run.

404
00:29:10.330 --> 00:29:17.860
<v Candidate>A CVP well above the wedge favours the right ventricle, but a localised clot behind the right atrium can do exactly the same thing.

405
00:29:18.360 --> 00:29:19.840
<v Examiner>How do you tell them apart?

406
00:29:20.340 --> 00:29:26.433
<v Candidate>Echo, and transoesophageal if the transthoracic windows are poor, which they usually are after sternotomy.

407
00:29:26.433 --> 00:29:33.810
<v Candidate>Tamponade after cardiac surgery is often localised, so you may not see the classic equalisation of pressures or a global effusion.

408
00:29:33.810 --> 00:29:38.353
<v Candidate>If there's a collection compressing a chamber, the surgeon reopens the chest.

409
00:29:38.353 --> 00:29:44.660
<v Candidate>If it's a dilated, hypokinetic right ventricle with the septum pushed left, I treat right ventricular failure.

410
00:29:45.160 --> 00:29:47.040
<v Examiner>And the treatment of the right ventricle?

411
00:29:47.540 --> 00:29:52.036
<v Candidate>Optimise preload, which usually means stopping fluid rather than giving it.

412
00:29:52.036 --> 00:30:04.276
<v Candidate>Reduce the right ventricular afterload: avoid hypoxia, hypercapnia and acidosis, keep the plateau pressure and PEEP modest, and start inhaled nitric oxide at around twenty parts per million or use inhaled prostacyclin.

413
00:30:04.276 --> 00:30:07.472
<v Candidate>Support contractility with milrinone or adrenaline.

414
00:30:07.472 --> 00:30:13.755
<v Candidate>Maintain systemic pressure with noradrenaline or vasopressin, so that the right coronary artery is perfused.

415
00:30:13.755 --> 00:30:15.380
<v Candidate>And keep AV synchrony.

416
00:30:15.880 --> 00:30:18.800
<v Examiner>You've left out one thing that kills these patients.

417
00:30:19.300 --> 00:30:21.549
<v Candidate>Intubation and positive pressure.

418
00:30:21.549 --> 00:30:30.159
<v Candidate>If she weren't already ventilated, induction could precipitate a right ventricular arrest, so it needs a cautious induction with vasopressor running.

419
00:30:30.159 --> 00:30:35.260
<v Candidate>And if she fails all of that, a temporary right ventricular assist device or VA ECMO.

420
00:30:35.760 --> 00:30:36.510
<v Examiner>Fine.

421
00:30:36.510 --> 00:30:40.029
<v Examiner>That's around seven out of ten as an extended answer.

422
00:30:40.029 --> 00:30:45.280
<v Examiner>The fix is the one from November: interpret every number before you treat anything.

423
00:30:45.280 --> 00:30:47.472
<v Examiner>Wedge, CVP, index, resistance.

424
00:30:47.472 --> 00:30:51.280
<v Examiner>Say what each one means, and the management writes itself.

425
00:30:52.180 --> 00:30:54.827
<v Tutor>That last line is the whole revisit.

426
00:30:54.827 --> 00:31:10.290
<v Tutor>Low output after cardiac surgery has been asked three times in the bank, with closely related questions on shock after cardiac surgery appearing as recently as the 2026 second sitting, and right ventricular dysfunction after cardiac surgery in 2025.

427
00:31:10.290 --> 00:31:13.900
<v Tutor>Next week's block is cardiac, so this is the bridge.

428
00:31:15.300 --> 00:31:16.728
<v Tutor>Time for the round-up.

429
00:31:16.728 --> 00:31:17.871
<v Tutor>Six items today.

430
00:31:17.871 --> 00:31:24.345
<v Tutor>One finishes this week's shock block, three finish the renal block, and two are beyond this week, from obstetrics and infection.

431
00:31:24.345 --> 00:31:28.820
<v Tutor>Each one gets what an examiner wants, the numbers, and the trial or guideline to name.

432
00:31:29.220 --> 00:31:33.984
<v Tutor>First, mechanical cardiac supports: ventricular assist devices and the Impella.

433
00:31:33.984 --> 00:31:36.722
<v Tutor>Oh's covers these in chapter twenty-eight.

434
00:31:36.722 --> 00:31:38.584
<v Tutor>Start with the categories.

435
00:31:38.584 --> 00:31:45.977
<v Tutor>Temporary percutaneous devices include the intra-aortic balloon pump, the Impella family of microaxial flow pumps, and VA ECMO.

436
00:31:45.977 --> 00:31:51.180
<v Tutor>Durable devices are implanted left ventricular assist devices, such as the HeartMate 3.

437
00:31:51.180 --> 00:31:56.875
<v Tutor>The Impella sits across the aortic valve and pumps blood from the left ventricle into the aorta.

438
00:31:56.875 --> 00:32:06.130
<v Tutor>It unloads the ventricle while adding forward flow, around three and a half to four litres a minute for the CP device, and more for the larger surgical versions.

439
00:32:06.130 --> 00:32:14.180
<v Tutor>Its complications are the exam points: haemolysis, limb ischaemia at the access site, bleeding, device migration, and aortic regurgitation.

440
00:32:14.580 --> 00:32:21.242
<v Tutor>The trial to name is DanGer Shock, published by Møller and colleagues in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2024.

441
00:32:21.242 --> 00:32:32.540
<v Tutor>Three hundred and sixty patients with ST-elevation myocardial infarction and cardiogenic shock, with a lactate above two point five millimoles per litre, were randomised to an Impella CP or standard care.

442
00:32:32.540 --> 00:32:40.854
<v Tutor>Death at one hundred and eighty days was forty-five point eight versus fifty-eight point five percent, with a hazard ratio of zero point seven four.

443
00:32:40.854 --> 00:32:43.572
<v Tutor>The number needed to treat was about eight.

444
00:32:43.572 --> 00:32:52.419
<v Tutor>But the composite of serious device complications was twenty-four versus six percent, and renal replacement therapy was forty-two versus twenty-seven percent.

445
00:32:52.419 --> 00:32:58.387
<v Tutor>It took ten years to recruit, it wasn't blinded, and it excluded comatose patients after cardiac arrest.

446
00:32:58.387 --> 00:33:08.460
<v Tutor>Quote it as: "the first trial of mechanical support to show a survival benefit in infarct-related cardiogenic shock, at the cost of more bleeding, limb ischaemia and kidney injury."

447
00:33:08.860 --> 00:33:13.301
<v Tutor>For a patient with a durable left ventricular assist device, know four things.

448
00:33:13.301 --> 00:33:20.376
<v Tutor>The pulse may be absent, so measure the mean pressure by Doppler, aiming for around seventy to eighty and certainly below ninety.

449
00:33:20.376 --> 00:33:26.212
<v Tutor>They're anticoagulated, and they develop an acquired von Willebrand deficiency, so GI bleeding is common.

450
00:33:26.212 --> 00:33:30.447
<v Tutor>Pump thrombosis shows up as a rising lactate dehydrogenase and haemolysis.

451
00:33:30.447 --> 00:33:33.958
<v Tutor>And right ventricular failure is the commonest early killer.

452
00:33:33.958 --> 00:33:40.982
<v Tutor>A suction event, where the ventricle collapses onto the inflow cannula, usually means hypovolaemia or right ventricular failure.

453
00:33:40.982 --> 00:33:44.700
<v Tutor>It's treated with volume and a lower pump speed, not more speed.

454
00:33:45.100 --> 00:33:52.008
<v Tutor>Second, the acute interstitial nephritis and glomerular causes of kidney injury, from Oh's chapter fifty-five.

455
00:33:52.008 --> 00:33:58.506
<v Tutor>Acute interstitial nephritis was asked as the third part of the 2016 second sitting, question thirteen.

456
00:33:58.506 --> 00:34:07.229
<v Tutor>A previously well woman on intravenous antibiotics for cellulitis becomes oliguric on day five, with an eosinophil count of four point three.

457
00:34:07.229 --> 00:34:11.737
<v Tutor>The answer was allergic interstitial nephritis due to the antibiotic.

458
00:34:11.737 --> 00:34:17.591
<v Tutor>The culprits are beta-lactams, proton pump inhibitors, NSAIDs, sulphonamides and rifampicin.

459
00:34:17.591 --> 00:34:24.030
<v Tutor>The classic triad of fever, rash and eosinophilia is present in only a minority, so don't wait for it.

460
00:34:24.030 --> 00:34:27.367
<v Tutor>The urine shows white cells and white cell casts.

461
00:34:27.367 --> 00:34:29.533
<v Tutor>Eosinophiluria is unreliable.

462
00:34:29.533 --> 00:34:31.641
<v Tutor>Biopsy is the gold standard.

463
00:34:31.641 --> 00:34:38.900
<v Tutor>Management is to stop the drug, and to consider early corticosteroids if function doesn't recover within a few days.

464
00:34:39.300 --> 00:34:42.485
<v Tutor>The glomerular syndromes come in two flavours.

465
00:34:42.485 --> 00:34:51.155
<v Tutor>Nephrotic syndrome means proteinuria above three and a half grams a day, albumin below thirty grams per litre, oedema, and hyperlipidaemia.

466
00:34:51.155 --> 00:34:58.763
<v Tutor>The ICU complication to name is venous thrombosis, including renal vein thrombosis, especially in membranous nephropathy.

467
00:34:58.763 --> 00:35:04.779
<v Tutor>Nephritic syndrome means haematuria with red cell casts, hypertension and a rising creatinine.

468
00:35:04.779 --> 00:35:10.500
<v Tutor>When it's rapidly progressive and the lungs bleed too, that's a pulmonary-renal syndrome.

469
00:35:10.500 --> 00:35:15.159
<v Tutor>Think of ANCA vasculitis and anti-glomerular basement membrane disease.

470
00:35:15.159 --> 00:35:23.829
<v Tutor>Send the ANCA, the anti-GBM antibody, complement, ANA and urine microscopy, and call nephrology early for the biopsy and induction therapy.

471
00:35:23.829 --> 00:35:27.840
<v Tutor>Plasma exchange still has a clear place in anti-GBM disease.

472
00:35:27.840 --> 00:35:35.448
<v Tutor>In ANCA vasculitis, the PEXIVAS trial didn't show that routine plasma exchange reduced death or end-stage kidney disease.

473
00:35:35.448 --> 00:35:41.700
<v Tutor>You saw part of this angle in October, in the pneumonia question that wanted an autoimmune screen.

474
00:35:42.100 --> 00:35:46.365
<v Tutor>Third, renal transplantation, from Oh's chapter one hundred and sixteen.

475
00:35:46.365 --> 00:35:49.564
<v Tutor>A transplant recipient in the ICU has four problems.

476
00:35:49.564 --> 00:35:53.990
<v Tutor>The graft, the immunosuppression, the infection, and the drug interactions.

477
00:35:53.990 --> 00:36:02.840
<v Tutor>Protect the graft: avoid nephrotoxins and hypovolaemia, and get an early Doppler ultrasound if function falls, to look at the artery, the vein and the ureter.

478
00:36:02.840 --> 00:36:12.544
<v Tutor>Tacrolimus and cyclosporin are themselves nephrotoxic and cause hypertension, hyperkalaemia, hypomagnesaemia and neurotoxicity, including posterior reversible encephalopathy.

479
00:36:12.544 --> 00:36:14.410
<v Tutor>So check the trough levels.

480
00:36:14.410 --> 00:36:17.129
<v Tutor>Azoles and macrolides push those levels up.

481
00:36:17.129 --> 00:36:19.688
<v Tutor>Rifampicin and phenytoin pull them down.

482
00:36:19.688 --> 00:36:23.900
<v Tutor>Patients on long-term prednisolone may need stress-dose hydrocortisone.

483
00:36:24.300 --> 00:36:26.777
<v Tutor>The infection timeline is a favourite.

484
00:36:26.777 --> 00:36:30.869
<v Tutor>In the first month, it's the usual surgical and hospital infections.

485
00:36:30.869 --> 00:36:36.362
<v Tutor>From one to six months, it's the opportunistic ones: cytomegalovirus, Pneumocystis, and fungi.

486
00:36:36.362 --> 00:36:42.177
<v Tutor>After six months, it's mostly community infections, unless the immunosuppression has been increased.

487
00:36:42.177 --> 00:36:47.723
<v Tutor>The College has asked about the timing of infection after transplant in the 2023 first sitting.

488
00:36:47.723 --> 00:36:52.354
<v Tutor>Whenever immunosuppression is reduced for sepsis, involve the transplant team.

489
00:36:52.354 --> 00:36:55.100
<v Tutor>Rejection is the price of getting it wrong.

490
00:36:55.500 --> 00:37:00.799
<v Tutor>While we're on the kidney, one more item that finishes the renal block: renal imaging.

491
00:37:00.799 --> 00:37:05.026
<v Tutor>Ultrasound comes first in nearly every case of acute kidney injury.

492
00:37:05.026 --> 00:37:09.254
<v Tutor>It's quick, it can be done at the bedside, and it uses no contrast.

493
00:37:09.254 --> 00:37:11.227
<v Tutor>It answers three questions.

494
00:37:11.227 --> 00:37:13.256
<v Tutor>First, is there obstruction?

495
00:37:13.256 --> 00:37:18.555
<v Tutor>Hydronephrosis means a post-renal cause, and you need a catheter or a nephrostomy now.

496
00:37:18.555 --> 00:37:20.809
<v Tutor>Second, how big are the kidneys?

497
00:37:20.809 --> 00:37:28.363
<v Tutor>Small, echogenic kidneys under about nine centimetres suggest chronic disease, which changes how you interpret the creatinine.

498
00:37:28.363 --> 00:37:30.336
<v Tutor>Third, is there blood flow?

499
00:37:30.336 --> 00:37:37.100
<v Tutor>A Doppler study looks for renal artery or vein thrombosis, especially after a transplant or an aortic procedure.

500
00:37:37.500 --> 00:37:39.096
<v Tutor>CT has its own place.

501
00:37:39.096 --> 00:37:41.791
<v Tutor>A non-contrast CT is the test for stones.

502
00:37:41.791 --> 00:37:48.559
<v Tutor>A CT angiogram is the test for renal artery dissection, aortic dissection with renal involvement, and renal trauma.

503
00:37:48.559 --> 00:37:52.245
<v Tutor>The fear of contrast has shrunk a lot over the last decade.

504
00:37:52.245 --> 00:38:00.828
<v Tutor>Large propensity-matched studies have suggested that much of what was called contrast nephropathy was kidney injury that would have happened anyway.

505
00:38:00.828 --> 00:38:04.624
<v Tutor>Don't withhold a diagnostic scan that will change management.

506
00:38:04.624 --> 00:38:11.061
<v Tutor>Instead, make sure the patient is euvolaemic, use the smallest dose that does the job, and stop nephrotoxins.

507
00:38:11.061 --> 00:38:15.517
<v Tutor>For MRI, the old worry was nephrogenic systemic fibrosis with gadolinium.

508
00:38:15.517 --> 00:38:23.660
<v Tutor>With modern macrocyclic agents, that risk is very low even with poor renal function, so again, the scan shouldn't be refused if it's needed.

509
00:38:24.060 --> 00:38:28.073
<v Tutor>Fourth, beyond this week: special considerations in the obstetric patient.

510
00:38:28.073 --> 00:38:30.569
<v Tutor>Oh's chapters eighty-eight and eighty-nine.

511
00:38:30.569 --> 00:38:37.029
<v Tutor>The curriculum links this item to twenty-nine past questions, which makes it one of the most examined areas in the syllabus.

512
00:38:37.029 --> 00:38:40.846
<v Tutor>Let's start with the physiology, because everything else hangs off it.

513
00:38:40.846 --> 00:38:44.468
<v Tutor>In the third trimester, what are the normal PaCO2 and bicarbonate?

514
00:38:44.468 --> 00:38:45.740
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

515
00:38:51.140 --> 00:38:56.293
<v Tutor>The PaCO2 is around thirty millimetres of mercury, because progesterone drives ventilation.

516
00:38:56.293 --> 00:39:01.811
<v Tutor>The bicarbonate falls to around eighteen to twenty-one millimoles per litre as renal compensation.

517
00:39:01.811 --> 00:39:03.841
<v Tutor>So the pH is slightly alkaline.

518
00:39:03.841 --> 00:39:08.786
<v Tutor>That means a PaCO2 of forty in a pregnant asthmatic is a sign of tiring, not normality.

519
00:39:08.786 --> 00:39:13.731
<v Tutor>And a mildly low bicarbonate is expected, not a pathological normal anion gap acidosis.

520
00:39:13.731 --> 00:39:18.364
<v Tutor>That was exactly the misread that cost you marks in the pregnancy gas in October.

521
00:39:18.364 --> 00:39:21.592
<v Tutor>Cardiac output rises by around forty to fifty percent.

522
00:39:21.592 --> 00:39:25.288
<v Tutor>Systemic vascular resistance and colloid osmotic pressure fall.

523
00:39:25.288 --> 00:39:29.712
<v Tutor>The functional residual capacity drops, so they desaturate fast at induction.

524
00:39:29.712 --> 00:39:33.460
<v Tutor>Their airways are oedematous, and they're at risk of aspiration.

525
00:39:33.860 --> 00:39:35.444
<v Tutor>Then the emergencies.

526
00:39:35.444 --> 00:39:41.941
<v Tutor>Aortocaval compression from about twenty weeks, so use manual left uterine displacement or a left lateral tilt.

527
00:39:41.941 --> 00:39:51.661
<v Tutor>In cardiac arrest, if the uterus is at or above the umbilicus, the resuscitative hysterotomy should begin by four minutes and the baby should be delivered by around five.

528
00:39:51.661 --> 00:39:56.521
<v Tutor>That's for the mother's sake, because emptying the uterus restores venous return.

529
00:39:56.521 --> 00:40:07.114
<v Tutor>For severe pre-eclampsia, the SOMANZ 2023 hypertension in pregnancy guideline treats a systolic of one hundred and sixty or more, or a diastolic of one hundred and ten or more, urgently.

530
00:40:07.114 --> 00:40:15.250
<v Tutor>Magnesium sulphate is given as a four-gram intravenous load over twenty minutes, then one gram an hour, halving the dose in renal impairment.

531
00:40:15.250 --> 00:40:17.816
<v Tutor>Check reflexes and urine output hourly.

532
00:40:17.816 --> 00:40:22.894
<v Tutor>Toxicity shows up as loss of reflexes and then a respiratory rate of twelve or below.

533
00:40:22.894 --> 00:40:26.935
<v Tutor>The treatment is ten millilitres of ten percent calcium gluconate.

534
00:40:26.935 --> 00:40:38.620
<v Tutor>The other headline obstetric emergencies are major haemorrhage, where a fibrinogen below two grams per litre predicts severe bleeding, amniotic fluid embolism, peripartum cardiomyopathy, and HELLP syndrome.

535
00:40:39.020 --> 00:40:41.094
<v Tutor>Each of those has a signature.

536
00:40:41.094 --> 00:40:44.916
<v Tutor>HELLP is haemolysis, elevated liver enzymes and low platelets.

537
00:40:44.916 --> 00:40:49.719
<v Tutor>It sits on the pre-eclampsia spectrum, and the definitive treatment is delivery.

538
00:40:49.719 --> 00:40:54.360
<v Tutor>The ICU complications are liver haematoma and rupture, kidney injury and DIC.

539
00:40:54.360 --> 00:41:02.930
<v Tutor>Amniotic fluid embolism classically presents at delivery as sudden collapse, hypoxia, and a consumptive coagulopathy that arrives early and severely.

540
00:41:02.930 --> 00:41:04.677
<v Tutor>Treatment is supportive.

541
00:41:04.677 --> 00:41:10.081
<v Tutor>Expect right ventricular failure first, replace fibrinogen aggressively, and consider ECMO.

542
00:41:10.081 --> 00:41:19.853
<v Tutor>Peripartum cardiomyopathy is new left ventricular systolic dysfunction, with an ejection fraction below forty-five percent, in late pregnancy or the months after delivery.

543
00:41:19.853 --> 00:41:24.984
<v Tutor>Treat it as heart failure, with drug choices adjusted for pregnancy and breastfeeding.

544
00:41:24.984 --> 00:41:30.279
<v Tutor>Bromocriptine is used in some centres, and mechanical support is a bridge in the sickest.

545
00:41:30.279 --> 00:41:38.467
<v Tutor>One last principle runs through every obstetric SAQ: there are two patients, and resuscitating the mother is the best treatment for the fetus.

546
00:41:38.467 --> 00:41:43.380
<v Tutor>Always mention early involvement of the obstetric, anaesthetic and neonatal teams.

547
00:41:43.380 --> 00:41:47.420
<v Tutor>Name the decision about delivery explicitly, including its timing.

548
00:41:47.820 --> 00:41:53.053
<v Tutor>Fifth, also beyond this week: antimicrobial use in the ICU, with antimicrobial pharmacology.

549
00:41:53.053 --> 00:41:54.885
<v Tutor>That's Oh's chapter twenty.

550
00:41:54.885 --> 00:41:58.129
<v Tutor>The syllabus links this to twenty-four past questions.

551
00:41:58.129 --> 00:42:00.066
<v Tutor>An examiner wants four ideas.

552
00:42:00.066 --> 00:42:01.531
<v Tutor>The first is timing.

553
00:42:01.531 --> 00:42:08.648
<v Tutor>The 2026 Surviving Sepsis Campaign guidelines have kept the urgency for definite sepsis and shock: give antibiotics immediately.

554
00:42:08.648 --> 00:42:15.294
<v Tutor>But for possible sepsis without shock, they've moved to a risk-based approach that allows a short period of assessment.

555
00:42:15.294 --> 00:42:20.580
<v Tutor>They also emphasise avoiding unnecessary anaerobic, antifungal and broad gram-negative cover.

556
00:42:20.980 --> 00:42:24.187
<v Tutor>The second idea is pharmacokinetics in the critically ill.

557
00:42:24.187 --> 00:42:30.504
<v Tutor>The volume of distribution goes up with capillary leak and fluid, so loading doses go up too, whatever the renal function.

558
00:42:30.504 --> 00:42:35.023
<v Tutor>Clearance can be reduced in kidney injury, or increased in augmented renal clearance.

559
00:42:35.023 --> 00:42:43.380
<v Tutor>That's a creatinine clearance above about one hundred and thirty millilitres a minute, common in young trauma and head injury patients, and it leads to underdosing.

560
00:42:43.780 --> 00:42:45.916
<v Tutor>The third is pharmacodynamics.

561
00:42:45.916 --> 00:42:51.539
<v Tutor>Beta-lactams are time-dependent: what matters is the time the free drug stays above the MIC.

562
00:42:51.539 --> 00:42:58.454
<v Tutor>Aminoglycosides are concentration-dependent, so give them once daily with a peak around eight to ten times the MIC.

563
00:42:58.454 --> 00:43:04.020
<v Tutor>For vancomycin, the target is the area under the curve, around four hundred to six hundred.

564
00:43:04.420 --> 00:43:06.167
<v Tutor>That leads to the trial.

565
00:43:06.167 --> 00:43:15.779
<v Tutor>BLING III, published by Dulhunty and colleagues in JAMA in 2024, randomised about seven thousand septic ICU patients to continuous or intermittent beta-lactam infusion.

566
00:43:15.779 --> 00:43:20.912
<v Tutor>Ninety-day mortality was twenty-four point nine versus twenty-six point eight percent.

567
00:43:20.912 --> 00:43:26.646
<v Tutor>That's an absolute difference of one point nine percent, and it wasn't statistically significant.

568
00:43:26.646 --> 00:43:30.250
<v Tutor>Clinical cure was clearly better with continuous infusion.

569
00:43:30.250 --> 00:43:35.547
<v Tutor>A companion meta-analysis in the same year found lower mortality with prolonged infusion.

570
00:43:35.547 --> 00:43:42.100
<v Tutor>So prolonged or continuous beta-lactam infusion is now a reasonable default, and you can defend it in an answer.

571
00:43:42.500 --> 00:43:45.148
<v Tutor>Put that together with this week's renal block.

572
00:43:45.148 --> 00:43:48.807
<v Tutor>How do you dose antibiotics on continuous renal replacement therapy?

573
00:43:48.807 --> 00:43:50.492
<v Tutor>Give the full loading dose.

574
00:43:50.492 --> 00:43:55.837
<v Tutor>Then the maintenance dose depends on the effluent rate and on how much of the drug is bound to protein.

575
00:43:55.837 --> 00:44:09.366
<v Tutor>At a typical effluent dose of twenty-five to thirty millilitres per kilogram per hour, clearance of a small, water-soluble, poorly protein-bound drug, like meropenem or piperacillin, is often similar to a creatinine clearance of around thirty to fifty millilitres a minute.

576
00:44:09.366 --> 00:44:14.373
<v Tutor>So the renal-failure doses in the drug handbook, which assume no clearance, will underdose them.

577
00:44:14.373 --> 00:44:17.791
<v Tutor>When circuit downtime is long, the patient is underdosed again.

578
00:44:17.791 --> 00:44:25.351
<v Tutor>Use therapeutic drug monitoring where you have it, for vancomycin and aminoglycosides routinely and for beta-lactams where your laboratory offers it.

579
00:44:25.351 --> 00:44:29.780
<v Tutor>Underdosing in the first forty-eight hours is the commoner and more dangerous error.

580
00:44:30.180 --> 00:44:32.574
<v Tutor>The fourth idea is stewardship.

581
00:44:32.574 --> 00:44:42.458
<v Tutor>That means cultures before antibiotics where it doesn't delay them, de-escalation at forty-eight to seventy-two hours, short courses, and source control.

582
00:44:42.458 --> 00:44:55.780
<v Tutor>When you write about resistance, which was asked in the 2017 second sitting, name the organisms: MRSA, VRE, ESBL and carbapenemase-producing Enterobacterales, and multi-resistant Pseudomonas and Acinetobacter.

583
00:44:57.180 --> 00:44:59.238
<v Tutor>That's the spiral for this week.

584
00:44:59.238 --> 00:45:01.192
<v Tutor>One thing to do before Monday.

585
00:45:01.192 --> 00:45:06.645
<v Tutor>Take a blank page and write out the issues list for the 2016 cardiac surgery question from memory.

586
00:45:06.645 --> 00:45:10.091
<v Tutor>Interpret every number before you write a single treatment.

587
00:45:10.091 --> 00:45:12.406
<v Tutor>Then check it against today's answer.

588
00:45:12.406 --> 00:45:19.505
<v Tutor>While you're there, write the desmopressin dose for diabetes insipidus and for uraemic bleeding, with the units, three times each.

589
00:45:19.505 --> 00:45:22.488
<v Tutor>Small habits like that are what protect the marks.

590
00:45:22.488 --> 00:45:23.980
<v Tutor>Thanks for listening.
